      "notes": "<h2> Mixtures & Separation Techniques</h2>\n  <h3> WHAT IS A MIXTURE?</h3>\n  <p>A mixture is a combination of two or more substances that are not chemically combined.</p>\n\n  <h3> TYPES OF MIXTURES</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Homogeneous mixture:</strong> Uniform throughout (e.g. salt solution)</li>\n    <li><strong>Heterogeneous mixture:</strong> Not uniform (e.g. sand and water)</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3> METHODS OF SEPARATION (WITH WHY)</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Magnetism:</strong> Removes magnetic materials → works because iron is magnetic</li>\n    <li><strong>Filtration:</strong> Separates insoluble solids → solid cannot pass through filter</li>\n    <li><strong>Evaporation:</strong> Removes liquid → liquid turns into vapor leaving solid</li>\n    <li><strong>Decantation:</strong> Pouring off liquid → based on density difference</li>\n    <li><strong>Sieving:</strong> Separates by particle size</li>\n    <li><strong>Distillation:</strong> Separates liquids by boiling point</li>\n    <li><strong>Chromatography:</strong> Separates based on movement in solvent</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Water purification → filtration</li>\n    <li>Salt production → evaporation</li>\n    <li>Oil refining → distillation</li>\n  </ul>\n  ",
