      "notes": "<h2>Acids</h2>\n<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water.</li>\n<li>They are usually corrosive and can burn tissues.</li>\n<li>Acids turn blue litmus paper red.</li>\n<li>They have a sour taste (but should never be tested in a lab).</li>\n<li>Acids can be strong or weak depending on the amount of ionization in water.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> ARRHENIUS DEFINITION</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An acid is a substance that increases the concentration of H⁺ ions in aqueous solution.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> DISSOCIATION MODEL</h3>\n<pre>\nHCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻\nH₂SO₄ → 2H⁺ + SO₄²⁻\nCH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻ (weak acid)\n</pre>\n\n<h3> COMMON ACIDS</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrochloric acid (HCl)</li>\n<li>Sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄)</li>\n<li>Nitric acid (HNO₃)</li>\n<li>Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> INDICATOR EFFECTS</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Blue litmus → Red</li>\n<li>Methyl orange → Red</li>\n<li>Phenolphthalein → Colourless</li>\n</ul>\n",
