      "notes": "<h2>Isomerism</h2>\n<h3> FOUNDATION NOTES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Isomerism occurs when compounds have the <b>same molecular formula</b> but different structural arrangements.</li>\n<li>These compounds are called <b>isomers</b>.</li>\n<li>Isomers have different physical and sometimes chemical properties.</li>\n<li>Differences arise due to <b>different atom arrangement</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> STRUCTURAL EXAMPLES (C₄H₁₀)</h3>\n\n<h4>1. Butane (straight chain)</h4>\n<pre>\nCH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃\n</pre>\n\n<h4>2. Isobutane (branched chain)</h4>\n<pre>\n      CH₃\n       |\nCH₃–CH–CH₃\n</pre>\n\n<h3> TYPES OF ISOMERISM (Basic Level)</h3>\n\n<h4>1. Structural Isomerism</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Different arrangement of atoms</li>\n<li>Different carbon chain structures</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>2. Chain Isomerism</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Different carbon skeleton (straight vs branched)</li>\n<li>Example: butane vs isobutane</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> WHY ISOMERS HAVE DIFFERENT PROPERTIES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Different shapes affect how molecules pack together</li>\n<li>Different intermolecular forces</li>\n<li>This affects boiling point, melting point, density</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> GENERAL IDEA</h3>\n<pre>\nSame formula → Different structure → Different properties\n</pre>\n</div>",
