      "notes": "<h2>Sulphur (S)</h2>\n\n<h3> GENERAL PROPERTIES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Yellow, brittle, solid non-metal at room temperature</li>\n<li>Found in volcanic regions and underground deposits</li>\n<li>Has atomic number 16</li>\n<li>Exists as S₈ molecules in its natural form</li>\n<li>Poor conductor of heat and electricity</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> CHEMICAL PROPERTIES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Burns in oxygen with a blue flame</li>\n<li>Forms acidic oxide sulphur dioxide (SO₂)</li>\n<li>Can be further oxidized to sulphur trioxide (SO₃)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"formula\">\nS + O₂ → SO₂  \n2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃  \nSO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄\n</div>\n\n<h3> INDUSTRIAL IMPORTANCE (CONTACT PROCESS)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>SO₂ is first produced by burning sulphur</li>\n<li>SO₂ is converted to SO₃ using a catalyst (V₂O₅)</li>\n<li>SO₃ is dissolved in water to form sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)</li>\n<li>Used conditions: high temperature (~450°C), pressure, catalyst</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> SULPHUR COMPOUNDS</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sulphur dioxide (SO₂):</b> toxic gas, bleaching agent, preserves food</li>\n<li><b>Sulphur trioxide (SO₃):</b> reacts with water to form acid</li>\n<li><b>Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄):</b> strong acid, highly corrosive, widely used industrial acid</li>\n<li><b>Hydrogen sulphide (H₂S):</b> rotten egg smell, toxic gas</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> USES OF SULPHURIC ACID</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Manufacture of fertilizers (e.g., ammonium sulphate)</li>\n<li>Car batteries (lead-acid batteries)</li>\n<li>Cleaning and metal processing</li>\n<li>Petroleum refining</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> SAFETY INFORMATION</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>SO₂ is harmful to lungs and causes acid rain</li>\n<li>H₂S is highly poisonous even at low concentrations</li>\n<li>H₂SO₄ causes severe burns on skin contact</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n<h2>Sulphuric Acid (H₂SO₄)</h2>\n\n<h3> GENERAL PROPERTIES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Strong mineral acid (one of the most important industrial acids)</li>\n<li>Colourless, oily, and highly corrosive liquid</li>\n<li>Very dense and heavy compared to water</li>\n<li>Hygroscopic: absorbs water from air and other substances</li>\n<li>Non-volatile acid (does not easily evaporate)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> CHEMICAL PROPERTIES</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Dissociates in water to produce H⁺ ions (strong acid behavior)</li>\n<li>Strong dehydrating agent (removes water from substances)</li>\n<li>Oxidizing agent when concentrated</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"formula\">\nSO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄\n</div>\n\n<h3> MANUFACTURE (CONTACT PROCESS SUMMARY)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Sulphur is burned to form SO₂</li>\n<li>SO₂ is oxidized to SO₃ using V₂O₅ catalyst</li>\n<li>SO₃ is absorbed in concentrated H₂SO₄ to form oleum</li>\n<li>Oleum is diluted to form sulphuric acid</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> USES OF SULPHURIC ACID</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Manufacture of fertilizers (ammonium sulfate, superphosphate)</li>\n<li>Car batteries (lead-acid batteries)</li>\n<li>Oil refining and petrochemicals</li>\n<li>Metal processing and cleaning (pickling metals)</li>\n<li>Manufacture of detergents, dyes, and explosives</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3> SAFETY AND HAZARDS</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Highly corrosive — causes severe skin burns</li>\n<li>Strong dehydrating agent — removes water from tissues (charring effect)</li>\n<li>Must always be added to water slowly (never water to acid)</li>\n</ul>\n",
